atural-language[111Gqueries;[120G80%[124Gempty[130Gon[133Gthe[137Ggolden[144Gset.[149GThat[154Gisn't[160Ga[162Gbug,[167Git's[172Gwhat[177GAND[181Gdoes[186Gto[189Ga[191Gsentence.[201GCombined[210Gwith[215Gthe[219Gearlier[227Gfinding[235Gthat[240Gyour[245Gcode[2C[1Bidentifiers ([38;2;177;185;249mrun_swarm[39m, [38;2;177;185;249mbudget_exhausted[39m) don't appear in a business-prompt vault[85Gat all, ex[96Gct search h[108Gs no addressable query population on this corpu[156G.[K[2C[1B[K[2C[1B[1mAnd this explains my earlier flip-flopping.[47G[22mYour[52G[38;2;177;185;249m_sparse_search[67G[39mwas[71Gnever[77Gexact[83Gsearch[90G—[92Git's[97GOR-matching[109Gon[112Gthe[116Gstemmed[124Genglish[132Gconfig.[140GThe[144G93.7%[150G"sparse[158Gwins[163Gnaming"[171Gnumber[178Gcame[183Gfrom[188Gloose[194GOR[197Gmatching[206Gon[209Ga[211Gleaked[218Gbenchmark,[229Gnot[233Gfrom[238Gexact[244Gmatching.[2C[1BTwo different techniques were wearing the same name in my analysis,[71Gwhich[77Gis why the story kept ch[102Gnging.[109GSeparating them, as you asked,[140Gmade it visible immediately.[K[2C[1B[K[2C[1B[1mWhat I'd take from this[2C[1B[22m[K[2C[1BVector[10Galone[16Gis[19Gthe[23Gstrongest[33Gsingle[40Gchannel[48Gon[51Gevery[57Gbenchmark[67Gyou[71Ghave.[77GThe[81Gexact[87Gchannel[95Gis[98Gworth[104Gkeeping[112Gin[115Gthe[119Gcodebase[128Gas[131Ga[133Gstandalone[144Gtool[149G—[151G[38;2;177;185;249mpython3 -m capillaries.search.channels exact "serve.py"[207G[39m—[209Gfor[213Gthe[217Gday[221Gyou[225Ggenuinely[235Gneed[240Gto[243Gfind[248Ga[2C[1Bliteral string,[19Gbut[23Gi[25G should[33Gnot sit in the[48Gdefault retriev[64Gl path, because it can't [91Gswer the queri[106Gs your users[119Gactually[128Gtype.[K[2C[1B[K[2C[1BNothing[11Gin[14Gthe[18Gexisting[27Gpipeline[36Gchanged.[45G[38;2;177;185;249mretriever.py[58G[39mstill[64Gdoes[69Gits[73G50/50[79GRRF;[84Gthese[90Gare[94Gnew,[99Gseparate[108Gmodules[116Gyou[120Gcan[124Grun[128Gand[132Greason[139Gabout[145Gindependently.[160GWhen[165Gyou[169Gwant[174Gto[177Gdecide[184Gwhether[192Ganything[201Gbeyond[208Gvector[215Gbelongs[223Gin[226Gthe[230Gdefault[238Gpath,[244Gthe[248Ghonest[2C[1Btest is[11Glabelling real queries in [38;2;177;185;249meval/plexus_queries.jsonl[39m — not[69Ganother ratio.[84G[K[2C[1B[K[1B[38;2;153;153;153m✻[3GCrunched for 3m 6s[1B[39m[K[1B[38;2;8;145;178m──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────[48;2;8;145;178m[38;2;0;0;0m fix-embedder-re-embed-corpus [49m[38;2;8;145;178m──[1B[39m❯ What was the exact logic for keyword search in RDB? What I meant here with RDB is searching in Postgres with the given query. Exact matching does happen for certain keywords; for example, asking "help me make a financial plan for next quarter" will[K[1B  focus on[16Gfinancial plan and next[40Gqu[44Gter to searc[57G with r[65Gther than [77Ge whole[85Gphrase which[98Gwill likely re[113Gurn nothing from Pos[134Ggres, while a query for ve[161Gtor search will go into RAG and f[196Gd[198Gw[200Gat was[207Gin common semantically across that entire[K[2C[1Bsearc[9G[K[61;1H[59;8H[?25h[?2026l[?2026h[?25l[H[7C[58Bh[61;1H[59;9H[?25h[?2026l[?2026h[?25l[59;10H[?25h[?2026l[?2026h[?25l[H[9C[58Bs[61;1H[59;11H[?25h[?2026l[?2026h[?25l[H[10C[58Bp[61;1H[59;12H[?25h[?2026l[?2026h[?25l[H[11C[58Ba[61;1H[59;13H[?25h[?2026l[?2026h[?25l[H[12C[58Bc[61;1H[59;14H[?25h[?2026l[?2026h[?25l[H[13C[58Be[61;1H[59;15H[?25h[?2026l]0;◑ fix-embedder-re-embed-corpus[?2026h[?25l[H[6G[K[5C[1B[K[2C[1B[38;2;153;153;153mRan [1m1[22m shell