ch — it's OR-matching on the stemm[122Gd engli[130Gh conf[137Gg. Th[143G 93.7% "sparse wins naming" number came[183Gfrom loose[194GOR matching on a leaked benchmark,[229Gnot[233Gfrom[238Gexact[244Gmatching.[2C[1BTwo[7Gdifferent[17Gtechniques[28Gwere[33Gwearing[41Gthe[45Gsame[50Gname[55Gin[58Gmy[61Ganalysis,[71Gwhich[77Gis[80Gwhy[84Gthe[88Gstory[94Gkept[99Gchanging.[109GSeparating[120Gthem,[126Gas[129Gyou[133Gasked,[140Gmade[145Git[148Gvisible[156Gimmediately.[2C[1B[K[2C[1B[1mWhat I'd take from this[27G[22m[K[2C[2BVector alone is the strongest single channel [49Gn every benchmark[67Gyou[71Gha[75G.[77GThe exact [89Gannel is worth keeping[112Gin the codebase a[130G a standalon[144Gtool — [38;2;177;185;249mpython3 -m capillaries.search.channels exact "serve.py"[39m —[209Gfor th[216G day you genuinely need to find a[K[2C[1Bliteral string, but it [27Ghould[33Gnot sit[41Gin the def[52Gult retriev[65G path, be[75Gause[80Git can't answer the quer[105Ges your users actually t[130Gpe.[K[2C[2BNothing in the existing[27Gpipeline[36Gchanged.[45G[38;2;177;185;249mretriever.py[58G[39mstill[64Gdoes[69Gits[73G50/50[79GRRF;[84Gthese[90Gare[94Gnew,[99Gseparate[108Gmodules[116Gyou[120Gcan[124Grun[128Gand[132Greason[139Gabout[145Gindependently.[160GWhen[165Gyou[169Gwant[174Gto[177Gdecide[184Gwhether[192Ganything[201Gbeyond[208Gvector[215Gbelongs[223Gin[226Gthe[230Gdefault[238Gpath,[244Gthe[248Ghonest[2C[1Btest[8Gis[11Glabelling[21Greal[26Gqueries[34Gin[37G[38;2;177;185;249meval/plexus_queries.jsonl[63G[39m—[65Gnot[69Ganother[77Gratio.[2C[1B[K[1B[38;2;153;153;153m✻[3GCrunched for 3m 6s[39m[K[2B[48;2;55;55;55m[38;2;80;80;80m❯ [38;2;255;255;255mWhat was the exact logic for keyword search in RDB? What I meant here with RDB is searching in Postgres with the given query. Exact matching does happen for certain keywords; for example, asking "help 