our.[76G[22mEven[81Gon[84Gthe[88Gcontaminated[101Gtitle[107Gset[111G—[113Gwhere[119Gthe[123Gquery[129Gstring[136Gis[139Gliterally[149Gplanted[157Gin[160Gthe[164Gtsvector[173Gat[176Gweight[183GA[185G—[187Gvector[194Gbeats[200Gboth[205Gkeyword[213Gvariants.[2C[1B[K[2C[1B[1mTrue exact search (AND, unstemmed) has almost no queries it can answer here.[80G[22m100% empty on[94Gnatural-language queries;[120G80% empty [131Gn[133Gthe golden se[147G. That isn't a bug, it's[172Gwhat[177GAND[181Gdoes[186Gto[189Ga[191Gsentence.[201GCombined[210Gwith[215Gthe[219Gearlier[227Gfinding[235Gthat[240Gyour[245Gcode[2C[1Bidentifiers[15G([38;2;177;185;249mrun_swarm[39m,[27G[38;2;177;185;249mbudget_exhausted[39m)[45Gdon't[51Gappear[58Gin[61Ga[63Gbusiness-prompt[79Gvault[85Gat[88Gall,[93Gexact[99Gsearch[106Ghas[110Gno[113Gaddressable[125Gquery[131Gpopulation[142Gon[145Gthis[150Gcorpus.[2C[1B[K[2C[1B[1mAnd this explains my earlier flip-flopping.[47G[22mYour[52G[38;2;177;185;249m_sparse_search[67G[39mwas[71Gnever[77Gexact[83Gsearch[90G—[92Git's[97GOR-matching[109Gon[112Gthe[116Gstemmed[124Genglish[132Gconfig.[140GThe[144G93.7%[150G"sparse[158Gwins[163Gnaming"[171Gnumber[178Gcame[183Gfrom[188Gloose[194GOR[197Gmatching[206Gon[209Ga[211Gleaked[218Gbenchmark,[229Gnot[233Gfrom[238Gexact[244Gmatching.[2C[1BTwo different tec[21Gniques w[30Gre[33Gwearing the same nam[54G in my an[64Glysis,[71Gwhich[77Gis why the story kept changing. Separa[116Ging th[123Gm, as you[133Gaske[138G, made it visible immediately.[K[2C[1B[K[2C[1B[1mWhat I'd take from this[2C[1B[22m[K[2C[1BV[5Gc[7Gor a[12Gon[15G is the strongest s[36Ggle channel on every benchmark you hav[75G.[77GThe exact[87Gchannel[95Gis[98Gworth[104Gkeeping[112Gin[115Gthe[119Gcodebase[128Gas[131Ga[133Gstandalone[144Gtool[149G—[151G[38;2;177;185;249mpython3 -m capillaries.search.ch